# Platform-First Development Standards (Eleventy + Sanity)

This is a mature production website. Prefer stability, incremental delivery, and simple solutions over rewrites.

## Core Decision Order

1. Follow web standards first (semantic HTML, accessibility, progressive enhancement).
2. Use Eleventy and Sanity native capabilities before writing custom logic.
3. Reuse existing project patterns/components before introducing new abstractions.
4. Add the smallest amount of custom code only when platform-native options are insufficient.
5. Avoid new dependencies unless the benefit is clear, repeated, and hard to achieve otherwise.

## Eleventy Expectations

- Prefer data cascade, collections, includes/layouts, filters, and shortcodes over bespoke systems.
- Keep templates readable; avoid complex business logic in template files.
- Reuse partials/includes instead of duplicating markup.
- Preserve existing URL and output conventions unless explicitly asked to change them.

## Sanity Expectations

- Prefer schema-driven solutions over ad hoc frontend transformations.
- Query only fields needed for the page/component; avoid over-fetching.
- Reuse document types and references before adding new schema shapes.
- Keep content structures editor-friendly and predictable.

## Frontend Expectations

- Default to semantic HTML and accessible interactions.
- Keep JavaScript minimal on static-first pages.
- Follow existing CSS architecture; avoid one-off styling patterns unless tightly scoped.

## Safety and Scope

- No major rewrites unless explicitly requested.
- Keep diffs small and localized to the feature being added.
- Do not refactor unrelated areas during feature work.
- For non-trivial changes: state assumptions, identify impacted templates/data sources, and call out risk.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Creating parallel systems where Eleventy/Sanity already provide a mechanism.
- Duplicating content logic across templates.
- Adding libraries for problems solvable with native platform features.
- Introducing clever abstractions for one-off needs.
